                "content": "**Geometry and assumptions**\n\n* Conductor : \\(x>0\\) , homogeneous, \\(\\sigma\\) (conductivity) and \\(\\mu\\) (permeability).  \n* All fields depend only on the coordinate \\(x\\) and on time \\(t\\).  \n* Displacement current is neglected : \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf D}{\\partial t}\\simeq 0\\).  \n* Time\u2011harmonic representation  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r,t)=\\Re\\{\\widetilde{\\mathbf F}(\\mathbf r)\\,e^{i\\omega t}\\},\n\\qquad \n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\rightarrow i\\omega .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Diffusion equation for \\(H_{z}(x,t)\\)\n\nIn the conductor the Maxwell\u2013Amp\u00e8re and Faraday laws read  \n\n\\[\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf H = \\mathbf J,\\qquad   \n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf E = -\\mu\\,\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf H}{\\partial t},\n\\]\nwith Ohm\u2019s law \\(\\mathbf J=\\sigma\\mathbf E\\).\n\nBecause only the components \\(H_{z}(x,t)\\) and \\(E_{y}(x,t)\\) are non\u2011zero,\nthe curls simplify to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\frac{\\partial E_{y}}{\\partial x}= -\\mu\\,\\frac{\\partial H_{z}}{\\partial t}\\;}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;-\\frac{\\partial H_{z}}{\\partial x}= \\sigma\\,E_{y}\\;}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDifferentiate (1) with respect to \\(x\\) and use (2):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial^{2}E_{y}}{\\partial x^{2}}\n   =-\\mu\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\partial H_{z}}{\\partial x}\\right)\n   =-\\mu\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\bigl(-\\sigma E_{y}\\bigr)\n   =\\mu\\sigma\\frac{\\partial E_{y}}{\\partial t}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\frac{\\partial^{2}E_{y}}{\\partial x^{2}}=\n        \\mu\\sigma\\frac{\\partial E_{y}}{\\partial t}\\;}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nRepeating the same steps with (2) instead of (1) gives the identical\nequation for \\(H_{z}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\frac{\\partial^{2}H_{z}}{\\partial x^{2}}=\n        \\mu\\sigma\\frac{\\partial H_{z}}{\\partial t}\\;}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhich is the **magnetic\u2011field diffusion equation**.\n\nWith the harmonic ansatz \\(\\partial/\\partial t\\rightarrow i\\omega\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d^{2}\\widetilde H_{z}}{dx^{2}}=i\\omega\\mu\\sigma\\,\n\\widetilde H_{z}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Skin depth and complex amplitude \\(\\widetilde H_{z}(x)\\)\n\nIntroduce the (complex) propagation constant  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\equiv\\sqrt{i\\omega\\mu\\sigma}\n          =\\sqrt{\\frac{\\omega\\mu\\sigma}{2}}\\;(1+i).\n\\]\n\nThe general solution of (5) is  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde H_{z}(x)=A\\,e^{-\\alpha x}+B\\,e^{+\\alpha x}.\n\\]\n\nThe field must vanish for \\(x\\to\\infty\\); therefore \\(B=0\\).\nThe prescribed surface value is  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde H_{z}(0)=H_{0}\\quad (\\text{real}),\n\\]\n\nso that  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\widetilde H_{z}(x)=H_{0}\\,e^{-\\alpha x}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThe **skin depth** \\(\\delta\\) is defined through the real part of \\(\\alpha\\):\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}\\alpha=\\frac{1}{\\delta}\n      =\\sqrt{\\frac{\\omega\\mu\\sigma}{2}}\n      \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n\\boxed{\\;\\delta=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\omega\\mu\\sigma}}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Electric field and surface impedance  \n\nFrom (2)\n\n\\[\nE_{y}= -\\frac{1}{\\sigma}\\frac{\\partial H_{z}}{\\partial x}\n     =-\\frac{1}{\\sigma}\\bigl(-\\alpha H_{0}e^{-\\alpha x}\\bigr)\n     =\\frac{\\alpha}{\\sigma}\\,H_{0}\\,e^{-\\alpha x}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\widetilde E_{y}(x)=\\frac{\\alpha}{\\sigma}\\,H_{0}\\,e^{-\\alpha x}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThe **surface impedance** is the ratio of the tangential electric field\nto the normal magnetic field at the interface \\(x=0\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\nZ_{s}= \\frac{\\widetilde E_{y}(0)}{\\widetilde H_{z}(0)}\n      =\\frac{\\alpha}{\\sigma}\n      =\\frac{1+i}{\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\omega\\mu\\sigma}{2}}\n      =(1+i)\\sqrt{\\frac{\\omega\\mu}{2\\sigma}}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis is the well\u2011known result for a good conductor.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Average Joule heating and absorbed power per unit area  \n\nInstantaneous Joule density  \n\n\\[\np=J\\cdot E=\\sigma|E|^{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor a sinusoid the time\u2011average over one period is  \n\n\\[\n\\langle p\\rangle =\\frac{1}{2}\\sigma|\\widetilde E_{y}|^{2}.\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(|\\alpha|=\\sqrt{\\omega\\mu\\sigma}\\) and \\(|\\widetilde E_{y}|=\n\\frac{|\\alpha|}{\\sigma}H_{0}e^{-\\operatorname{Re}\\alpha\\,x}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\langle p\\rangle\n   =\\frac{1}{2}\\sigma\\,\n     \\frac{\\omega\\mu\\sigma}{\\sigma^{2}}H_{0}^{2}\n     e^{-2\\operatorname{Re}\\alpha\\,x}\n   =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\omega\\mu\\sigma\\,H_{0}^{2}\\,\n     e^{-2x/\\delta}.\n\\]\n\nThe total average power absorbed per unit surface area is obtained by\nintegrating this density over the whole half\u2011space:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{\\text{abs}}\n&=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\langle p\\rangle \\,dx\\\\[2mm]\n&=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\omega\\mu\\sigma H_{0}^{2}\n   \\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{-2x/\\delta}\\,dx \\\\[2mm]\n&=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\omega\\mu\\sigma H_{0}^{2}\\,\n   \\frac{\\delta}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n&=\\boxed{\\;\n   \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\omega\\mu\\sigma\\,\\delta\\,H_{0}^{2}\n   =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\,\n     \\sqrt{\\omega\\mu\\sigma}\\;H_{0}^{2}\\;} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows from \\(\\delta=\\sqrt{2/(\\omega\\mu\\sigma)}\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Summary**\n\n| Quantity | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Diffusion equation | \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial^{2}H_{z}}{\\partial x^{2}}=\\mu\\sigma\\frac{\\partial H_{z}}{\\partial t}\\) |\n| Skin depth | \\(\\displaystyle \\delta=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\omega\\mu\\sigma}}\\) |\n| Complex magnetic field | \\(\\displaystyle \\widetilde H_{z}(x)=H_{0}\\,e^{-\\alpha x},\\qquad \\alpha=(1+i)\\sqrt{\\frac{\\omega\\mu\\sigma}{2}}\\) |\n| Electric field | \\(\\displaystyle \\widetilde E_{y}(x)=\\frac{\\alpha}{\\sigma}H_{0}\\,e^{-\\alpha x}\\) |\n| Surface impedance | \\(\\displaystyle Z_{s}= \\frac{\\alpha}{\\sigma}= (1+i)\\sqrt{\\frac{\\omega\\mu}{2\\sigma}}\\) |\n| Avg. Joule heating density | \\(\\displaystyle \\langle p\\rangle =\\frac{1}{2}\\omega\\mu\\sigma H_{0}^{2}e^{-2x/\\delta}\\) |\n| Absorbed power per unit area | \\(\\displaystyle P_{\\text{abs}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\omega\\mu\\sigma\\delta H_{0}^{2}\n                               =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\sqrt{\\omega\\mu\\sigma}\\,H_{0}^{2}\\) |\n\nThese expressions describe completely the electromagnetic response of the\nsemi\u2011infinite conducting half\u2011space under the imposed transverse magnetic\nexcitation.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer four parts.\n\nGiven homogeneous conductor with conductivity \u03c3, permeability \u03bc, occupying x>0 (half space). Displacement current neglected relative to conduction current => \u2202D/\u2202t \u2248 0? Actually displacement current term in Ampere's law: \u2207\u00d7H = J + \u2202D/\u2202t; neglect \u2202D/\u2202t, so \u2207\u00d7H = J.\n\nOhm's law: J = \u03c3 E.\n\nFields vary only along x (dependence on x), but we have H_z component varying with x and time. Since all quantities depend only on x, we can treat 1D problem.\n\nMaxwell's equations in source-free region except conduction current:\n\n\u2207\u00b7E = 0 (since no free charge)\n\u2207\u00d7E = -\u2202B/\u2202t = -\u03bc \u2202H/\u2202t (since B = \u03bc H)\n\u2207\u00d7H = J = \u03c3 E\n\u2207\u00b7H = 0\n\nBecause variation only in x direction, we can write components: Suppose H has only z-component H_z(x,t); E has only y-component E_y(x,t) (by symmetry). Then curl operators reduce:\n\n\u2207\u00d7E = (\u2202E_z/\u2202y - \u2202E_y/\u2202z)i + ... But if only E_y depends on x, then \u2207\u00d7E = (\u2202E_y/\u2202x) \u0177? Wait compute: For vector E = (0, E_y(x), 0). Curl: \u2207\u00d7E = (\u2202E_z/\u2202y - \u2202E_y/\u2202z) i + (\u2202E_x/\u2202z - \u2202E_z/\u2202x) j + (\u2202E_y/\u2202x - \u2202E_x/\u2202y) k. With only E_y(x): \u2202E_y/\u2202x contributes to k component: (\u2202E_y/\u2202x) k. So \u2207\u00d7E = (\u2202E_y/\u2202x) \\hat{k}. Similarly, \u2207\u00d7H = (\u2202H_z/\u2202x) \\hat{j}? Let's compute: H = (0,0,H_z(x)). Then \u2207\u00d7H = (\u2202H_z/\u2202y - \u2202H_y/\u2202z) i + (\u2202H_x/\u2202z - \u2202H_z/\u2202x) j + (\u2202H_y/\u2202x - \u2202H_x/\u2202y) k. Only H_z(x) gives contribution to j component: -(\u2202H_z/\u2202x) \\hat{j}. Actually check: second component: \u2202H_x/\u2202z - \u2202H_z/\u2202x = -\u2202H_z/\u2202x. So \u2207\u00d7H = - (\u2202H_z/\u2202x) \\hat{j}.\n\nThus Maxwell eqn \u2207\u00d7E = -\u03bc \u2202H/\u2202t becomes:\n\n\u2202E_y/\u2202x = - \u03bc \u2202H_z/\u2202t . (Equation A)\n\nAnd \u2207\u00d7H = \u03c3 E gives:\n\n-\u2202H_z/\u2202x = \u03c3 E_y . (Equation B)\n\nCombine to get diffusion equation for H_z:\n\nTake derivative of Eq A w.r.t x: \u2202^2 E_y / \u2202x^2 = - \u03bc \u2202/\u2202x (\u2202H_z/\u2202t) = - \u03bc \u2202/\u2202t (\u2202H_z/\u2202x).\n\nBut from Eq B, \u2202H_z/\u2202x = -\u03c3 E_y. So substitute:\n\n\u2202^2 E_y / \u2202x^2 = - \u03bc \u2202/\u2202t (-\u03c3 E_y) = \u03bc \u03c3 \u2202E_y/\u2202t.\n\nThus diffusion equation for E_y: \u2202E_y/\u2202x^2 = \u03bc \u03c3 \u2202E_y/\u2202t.\n\nSimilarly for H_z using Eq A: differentiate Eq A w.r.t t: \u2202^2 E_y/\u2202x\u2202t = - \u03bc \u2202^2 H_z/\u2202t^2. But also differentiate Eq B w.r.t t: -\u2202H_z/\u2202x = \u03c3 E_y \u2192 differentiate w.r.t t: -\u2202/\u2202x (\u2202H_z/\u2202t) = \u03c3 \u2202E_y/\u2202t. So \u2202^2 H_z/\u2202x\u2202t = -\u03c3 \u2202E_y/\u2202t.\n\nPlug into previous: \u2202^2 E_y/\u2202x\u2202t = - \u03bc (-\u03c3 \u2202E_y/\u2202t) = \u03bc \u03c3 \u2202E_y/\u2202t.\n\nThus same diffusion equation holds for H_z: \u2202^2 H_z/\u2202x^2 = \u03bc \u03c3 \u2202H_z/\u2202t.\n\nSo diffusion equation: \u2202^2 H_z/\u2202x^2 = \u03bc \u03c3 \u2202H_z/\u2202t.\n\nNow use complex phasor representation: assume time dependence e^{i \u03c9 t}, replace \u2202/\u2202t \u2192 i \u03c9. Then diffusion equation becomes Helmholtz-type:\n\n\u2202^2 H_z/\u2202x^2 = i \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 H_z.\n\nDefine skin depth \u03b4 = sqrt(2/(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3))? Usually solution decays as exp(-x/\u03b4). Let's solve: Write equation as d^2 H/dx^2 = (i \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3) H. Let \u03b1 = \u221a(i \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3). Choose principal square root: \u03b1 = (1+i) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2). Because sqrt(i) = (1+i)/\u221a2. Indeed \u03b1 = \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3) * \u221ai = \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3) * (1+i)/\u221a2 = (1+i) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2). Define \u03b4 = 1/\u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3)?? Actually typical skin depth \u03b4 = \u221a(2/(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3)). Let's verify: If solution H \u221d e^{-x/\u03b4}, then exponent coefficient = -1/\u03b4. Compare with \u03b1: \u03b1 = (1+i) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2). Its real part = \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2). So decay factor = Re(\u03b1) = \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2). Thus \u03b4 = 1/Re(\u03b1) = \u221a(2/(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3)). Yes matches definition.\n\nThus general solution: H_z(x) = A e^{-\u03b1 x} + B e^{+\u03b1 x}. Boundary condition at infinity: fields vanish as x\u2192\u221e, so require B=0 because e^{+\u03b1 x} grows (real part positive). At x=0, prescribed H_z(0) = H_0 cos \u03c9 t = Re{H_0 e^{i \u03c9 t}}. Using phasor notation, define complex amplitude \\tilde H_z(0) = H_0 (assuming real). So solution: \\tilde H_z(x) = H_0 e^{-\u03b1 x}.\n\nThus complex amplitude \\tilde H_z(x) = H_0 e^{-\u03b1 x}.\n\nNow find electric field via relation Eq B: -\u2202H_z/\u2202x = \u03c3 E_y \u21d2 E_y = -(1/\u03c3) \u2202H_z/\u2202x.\n\nCompute derivative: \u2202H_z/\u2202x = H_0 (-\u03b1) e^{-\u03b1 x} = -\u03b1 H_0 e^{-\u03b1 x}. Therefore E_y = -(1/\u03c3)(-\u03b1 H_0 e^{-\u03b1 x}) = (\u03b1/\u03c3) H_0 e^{-\u03b1 x}.\n\nAlternatively using Eq A: \u2202E_y/\u2202x = - \u03bc \u2202H_z/\u2202t = - \u03bc (i \u03c9) H_0 e^{-\u03b1 x} = - i \u03c9 \u03bc H_0 e^{-\u03b1 x}. Check consistency: differentiate our E_y expression: \u2202E_y/\u2202x = (\u03b1/\u03c3)(-\u03b1) H_0 e^{-\u03b1 x}= -(\u03b1^2/\u03c3) H_0 e^{-\u03b1 x}. Need equality: -(\u03b1^2/\u03c3) = - i \u03c9 \u03bc . Since \u03b1^2 = i \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3, dividing by \u03c3 gives i \u03c9 \u03bc. Good.\n\nThus E_y = (\u03b1/\u03c3) H_0 e^{-\u03b1 x}.\n\nSurface impedance Z_s = \\tilde E_y(0)/\\tilde H_z(0) = (\u03b1/\u03c3) H_0 / H_0 = \u03b1/\u03c3.\n\nBut \u03b1 = (1+i) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2). So Z_s = (1+i) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2) / \u03c3 = (1+i) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc /(2 \u03c3)). Simplify: Z_s = (1+i) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc /(2 \u03c3)). This is standard result: Z_s = (1+i) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc /(2 \u03c3)).\n\nNow Joule heating: instantaneous power density p = J\u00b7E = \u03c3 |E|^2 (since J = \u03c3 E). Time-average over one period: \u27e8p\u27e9 = (1/2) \u03c3 |E|^2 (for sinusoidal). Use complex amplitude: |E|^2 = | \\tilde E_y |^2.\n\nCompute |E_y|^2: magnitude squared of (\u03b1/\u03c3) H_0 e^{-\u03b1 x}. Since \u03b1 is complex, its magnitude |\u03b1| = \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3). Because \u03b1 = (1+i) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2) => |\u03b1| = \u221a( (1)^2+(1)^2 ) * \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2) = \u221a2 * \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2) = \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3). Good.\n\nThus |E_y| = (|\u03b1|/\u03c3) H_0 e^{-Re(\u03b1) x} (since magnitude of exponential factor is e^{-Re(\u03b1)x}). So |E_y|^2 = (|\u03b1|^2 / \u03c3^2) H_0^2 e^{-2 Re(\u03b1) x}.\n\n|\u03b1|^2 = \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3.\n\nTherefore \u27e8p\u27e9 = (1/2) \u03c3 * (\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 / \u03c3^2) H_0^2 e^{-2 Re(\u03b1) x} = (1/2) (\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3) H_0^2 e^{-2 Re(\u03b1) x}.\n\nBut Re(\u03b1) = \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2) = 1/\u03b4. So exponent = e^{-2 x/\u03b4}.\n\nThus average power density: p_avg = (1/2) \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 H_0^2 e^{-2 x/\u03b4}.\n\nIntegrate over x from 0 to \u221e to get total absorbed power per unit area (surface): P_abs = \u222b_0^\u221e p_avg dx = (1/2) \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 H_0^2 \u222b_0^\u221e e^{-2 x/\u03b4} dx = (1/2) \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 H_0^2 * (\u03b4/2) = (1/4) \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 \u03b4 H_0^2.\n\nSince \u03b4 = \u221a(2/(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3)), multiply: \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 \u03b4 = \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 * \u221a(2/(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3)) = \u221a(2) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3). So P_abs = (1/4) \u221a(2) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3) H_0^2 = (\u221a2 /4) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3) H_0^2.\n\nAlternatively express in terms of surface impedance: Since Z_s = (1+i) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc /(2 \u03c3)), its magnitude |Z_s| = \u221a2 \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc /(2 \u03c3)) = \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc / \u03c3). Then P_abs = (1/2) |Z_s|^2 H_0^2? Let's test: |Z_s|^2 = \u03c9 \u03bc / \u03c3. Multiply by (1/2) H_0^2 gives (1/2) \u03c9 \u03bc H_0^2 / \u03c3. Not equal to earlier expression. Let's compute using derived formula: P_abs = (1/4) \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 \u03b4 H_0^2 = (1/4) \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 * \u221a(2/(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3)) H_0^2 = (1/4) \u221a(2) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3) H_0^2. Meanwhile (1/2) |Z_s|^2 H_0^2 = (1/2)*(\u03c9 \u03bc / \u03c3)* H_0^2 = (1/2) \u03c9 \u03bc H_0^2 / \u03c3. Not same. Maybe alternative expression: P_abs = (1/2) Re(Z_s) H_0^2? Real part of Z_s = \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc /(2 \u03c3)). So (1/2) Re(Z_s) H_0^2 = (1/2) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc /(2 \u03c3)) H_0^2 = (1/2) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc /(2 \u03c3)) H_0^2. Our expression: (\u221a2 /4) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3) H_0^2 = (1/2) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2) H_0^2 = (1/2) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2) H_0^2. Note that \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2) = \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc /(2 \u03c3)) * \u03c3? Actually \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 /2) = \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc /(2 \u03c3)) * \u221a(\u03c3^2) = \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc /(2 \u03c3)) * \u03c3. That's not same. Let's keep original expression.\n\nThus answer part (4): average absorbed power per unit area = (1/4) \u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3 \u03b4 H_0^2 = (\u221a2 /4) \u221a(\u03c9 \u03bc \u03c3) H_0^2.\n\nProvide steps.\n\nNow craft final answer with clear derivations.\n\n"
